{SFX: sliding stone}

{LOOT}

The climb feels easy. You're halfway up when suddenly both of your footholds crumble simultaneously. You grab a jutting rock in panic, but it snaps away. You land with a sickening *thwack* onto the ledge below.

It could've been worse. On the plus side, you think you've figured out how to read the cliff to find better footholds.

{SOUND: PC climbing, then rocks crumbling and the impact of hitting the ledge a few meters below.}

{DAMAGE}

For a long moment, Omahdon looks like he's about to punch you, but then he turns away. "You're right. My Perseia was the one who slept. The one who woke was a stranger to me, and I have tortured both her and myself by trying to force her reality to match my fantasy. I have been a fool, and worse, a villain. She... she will not see me again."

He bows to you. "Please take this. You have saved me from worse villainy. That is a gift beyond price."

{SCRIPT: Need some kind of reward.}

Your blade slides through them like melting butter. Their screams turn to gurgling death rattles, while their blood sprays you head to toe.

Then it's over. They are gone - truly released - and the path before you is clear.

{SOUND: Horrible screams.}

It takes great effort to hack and saw through them. They scream and shriek with each cut of your blade, continuing long after they should have passed out from the pain. Blood sprays in every direction, covering you entirely.

Then it's over. They are gone - truly released - and the path before you is clear.

{SOUND: Horrible screams, preferably worse than the other two flesh anchors.}

You try to rip yourself away, but the Bloom latches onto you both mentally and physically. It saps some of your strength, and a buzzing hammers at your mind like laughter.

Finally it lets you go and you fall back on your hands. Your breathing comes heavy. The Bloom took something from you, but apparently only out of spite. The patch looks the same as before.

{SCRIPT: Conditional required: Player has abused the Bloom.}

He frowns, the tips of his sharp teeth peeking between his lips. "Do not presume to throw my word back on me, castoff. I said I would accept an item of *equal* worth. And while that item is indeed interesting, it is not the equivalent of the Annulet."

He looks at it a moment in thought, then says, "I would be willing to take it from you, though. My firm offer is [Global: c4101_DracogenCortexReward] shins."

{MONEY}

It seems that he intends to manipulate the Bloom's transdimensional nature to test his Murine Dreamtime Hypothesis. Such meddling, of course, cannot be tolerated, so as he natters on, you slip the Sunspot Totem into the sulcus. 

It seems implausible that the codger will ever complete his dimensional anchor, but now, at least, you can be sure that it will not interfere with your own plans in the Bloom.

{LOOT: The item described here must be the same as that dropped in node 27.}

This merecaster is much lighter than others you've seen. You heft it experimentally in your hands and notice that it's nearly transparent, but only when it's closer to your face. 

You feel it pulling at you, urging you to enter the mere held within.
{GAVIN: This needs jazziness.}

As quickly as you can, you lunge for the opening in the construct's armor... but one of the construct's blade-arms darts out with sudden, surgical precision. It slashes open your forearm, and you cry out, jerking your arm away. 

{SCRIPT: Damage the player.}

{DAMAGE}

You reach down and pick up the head. On close inspection, it appears to be made of metal and ivory, fused or grown together by some unknown means, yet it is surprisingly light. A few lights flicker from within, suggesting that the construct's consciousness is still alive inside.

You also pick up the device that fell from its chest, which appears to be a very ancient slugspitter. 

{SCRIPT: Kill Waits-for-prey when this node exits and give player its head. Also give player a damaged or reparable slugspitted.}

{LOOT}

You reach up and grasp the head. On close inspection, it appears to be made of metal and ivory, fused or grown together by some unknown means, yet it is surprisingly light. As you pull it toward you, the body topples over, lifeless.

Reaching inside its body, you also find the device it had told you about and put it in your pack.

{SCRIPT: Give player a power source.}

"When you went down, her thugs moved in. They killed everyone in the fortress who had sworn for you. They hunted us down in the belly of the Bloom. They cast us through Maws under unfamiliar suns. I fled into the city with a few of your lieutenants, and we had to kill some of our friends to get through the gates. And once we'd settled in up there, she started hunting us in the city, too."

She shakes her head. "She cast aside years of tradition to hunt me. I'd have sworn loyalty when you died."

{MATKINA this is part of her history}

"I... don't know. The patrol schedules have been spotty lately, and we had another one go missing just the other day - " He clamps his mouth shut. The mutants leer appreciatively at this newfound alibi, and the enforcer hastily adds, "-but the Memovira spared no expense to find them."

No one believes him.

{SCRIPT:  Add a negative asset to the tasks to convince the mutants to stop. The player just inadvertently gave them an alibi to attack these guys.}

You grit your teeth against the pain, struggling against your body's natural instinct to cast it out, while the tongues tear the memory from the depths of your unconscious. At last it pulls free, unraveling itself before your eyes...

{SCRIPT: Int damage currently set to 3 - should be same as other cases when the player allows Bloom-tongues to take a memory.}

{DAMAGE}

You close your eyes and concentrate upon the pain, defying your body's instinct to cast it out. Dimly you are aware of the Bloom's presence in your mind, pulling at a memory hidden deep in your unconscious, like a stubborn tooth that refuses to be torn out.

With a final gout of searing agony, the memory bursts into your conscious mind...

{SCRIPT: Int damage currently set to 3 - should be same as other cases when the player allows Bloom-tongues to take a memory.}

{DAMAGE}

The pain grows sharper still, like a hot knife behind your eyes. A memory bursts into your conscious mind...

{SCRIPT: Int damage currently set to 3 - should be same as other cases when the player allows Bloom-tongues to take a memory.}

{DAMAGE}

Finally they fixate upon Seria, the adopted daughter of Orseolo. Perhaps they are drawn to the torment that she suffered in death, or to the torment of her foster-father... for she is truly the last vestige of his mind. 

You can see her as the Bloom-voices do - cowering in the darkness as they chitter and hiss all around her.

{SCRIPT: Seria reflection is present in Labyrinth.}

The circular symbol dims, and the pulsing rhythm of symbols marching into the image cease.

The low hum that permeates the cave fades then grows completely silent.

{SOUND: The humming sound should stop here.}

The screen cracks and shatters under your mighty blow. You continue, smashing the frame and the housing until the machine itself goes dark.

The devices in the rest of the room go dark as well. The humming that once permeated the cave grows eerily silent.

{SOUND: Stop the low hum.}

A picture of the pod appears. The lid on the image is closed and flashing red. Several other places on the image of the pod flash red as well.

The text says, "Stasis pod empty. Error: Self-diagnostic failed. Cannot open stasis pod. Primary functions offline. Data integrity: 67%."

{GEORGE: The text is written such that the player doesn't get any ideas about opening or using it, HOWEVER if we ever have need of a stasis pod in a side quest, we could open this up to be of use elsewhere. Alternatively, this thing could actually have been used to incubate synthetic living crystals, or even to hatch the crystalvores. -JF/AH}

"We met with his agent, a graying, hirsute, overbuilt specimen with unsightly slashes of scar tissue across his brutish cheekbones." 

The automaton shakes its metal head in distaste, contrasting somewhat oddly with the fixed gaze of the severed head below. "Apologies. We find it odd that such an illustrious personage would use so unsuitable a specimen as their respresentative."

{ADAM: This node will need to be updated with Tash's description. Sharepoint doesn't have a whole lot on the guy besides "stocky", and I couldn't get anything from either version of the Kholn Village Mere.}

You lunge at the Decanted. Its hand darts to a series of rounded buttons on the front of its armored shell. 

A radiant pulse of light staggers you. When your vision clears, the creature is gone. 

{SCRIPT: B-priority, this will start a Tussle (and the RanItOff flag will get set only if the player wins).}

[This is the marker Matak was looking for. You found it. Good job.]

{SCRIPT: Matak is in party.}
